There is a moment, somewhere past the halfway mark of a long manuscript, when the
book grows larger than the head holding it. You can no longer remember whether the
war ended in the spring or the autumn, whether your detective has met the widow
yet, whether chapter nine still drags the way it did three revisions ago. The book
knows these things. You wrote them. But knowing and *holding* are different, and no
writer holds a whole book at once.

This is a book about the second kind of knowing. Inkhaven cannot write your novel —
and would not try — but it can *hold your book for you*: remember its facts, watch
its continuity, track who knows what, read it the way a stranger would, listen to
its voices, and tell you, draft to draft, whether it is getting better. Every one
of these is a way of *knowing your book*, and this guide is a tour of all of them.

#section("What this book covers")

Inkhaven's knowledge features are not one tool but a family, each answering a
different question you would otherwise have to answer from memory:

#screen(caption: "The questions — and who answers them")[```
  the Facts bible  │ Is this fact right, sourced?
  the graph        │ How does it all connect?
  SENTINEL         │ Does the continuity hold?
  KEN              │ Could they know this yet?
  LECTOR           │ How does the whole thing read?
  CHORUS           │ Do the voices differ?
  CHRONICLE        │ Did the revision help?
  ─────────────────┼─ these ask, they don't answer
  Inner Socrates   │ Have I earned this claim?
  Inner Editor     │ Is the prose doing its job?
  Inner Theologian │ Is the moral weight honest?
  Inner Poet       │ Does the verse keep its form?
```]

The first group *tells you what is true* — the state of your book. The last group —
the *inner readers* — does something quieter and stranger: it *asks whether you have
earned it*. A Socratic questioner will not fix your argument; it will name the premise
you never said aloud. Both are ways of knowing your book, and Part VI is given to the
readers who question.

You do not have to use all of them, and few books need every one. Read the chapter
for the question that is nagging you today; the book is arranged so each stands on
its own.

#section("Who this book is for")

This guide assumes *no prior knowledge* — of Inkhaven, or of the craft-analysis
ideas behind these features. Every term is introduced the first time it is needed,
in a marked box:

#term("Advisory")[
  Every feature in this book is *advisory*. It observes, measures, and reports; it
  never edits your prose on its own. The reader points; the author decides. When a
  change is made, you see it as a diff and confirm it, and your old words are always
  one keystroke back.
]

It also keeps two kinds of author in view — the novelist and the non-fiction writer
— because the intelligence serves both, though it serves them differently:

#two_track(
  [The novelist leans hardest on *continuity*, *who-knows-what*, and *voice* — the
  machinery of a world with secrets and a cast who must each sound like themselves.],
  [The non-fiction writer leans hardest on the *facts bible* and the *graph* — the
  machinery of claims that must be right, sourced, and coherent with each other.],
)

#section("One promise about cost")

The word "intelligence" makes writers reach for their wallet, expecting a meter to
spin every time the tool thinks. It does not. Almost everything in this book is
*deterministic and free* — it reads your manuscript and its own structure, with no
model involved. Where a language model genuinely helps — the subtle continuity
break, a synthetic first read — it is always *explicit and cost-capped*: you ask
for it, you see the estimate, and the default is off. Cost *informs*; it never
surprises.

So: turn the page, and let us begin with the ground the whole book stands on — the
facts it did not invent.

